    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d593">

                <pb facs="0928" n="808" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg808"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/3962: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Acting Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-09-08T16:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">September 8,
                                1922—4 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received September 8—9:15
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>364. Your 209, September 1, 5 p.m. Legation has repeatedly brought this situation
                    to the attention of the Foreign Office, Ministry of Finance and Ministry of
                    Communication[s]. Payments continue to be made fairly regularly on 14,000,000
                    treasury notes,<anchor n="74" xml:id="d593-f1"/> consolidated internal loans,
                    and now on 96,000,000 while Government is indifferent to defaulted foreign
                    obligations. Full report follows.<anchor n="73" xml:id="d593-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d593-f1" n="74" xml:id="d593-f1-content">
                <p>See telegram no. 19, Jan. 21, from the Minister in China, <ref target="#pg762">p.
